"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings include a pathological stage of pT3a with extraprostatic extension and negative surgical margins, which are favorable. The seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. The biopsy revealed Gleason 3+4 adenocarcinoma with perineural invasion. A key uncertainty is the nodal status, as no lymph nodes were removed, and missing nodal sampling widens this uncertainty. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 6.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the absence of lymphovascular invasion. The difference between the biopsy finding and the prostatectomy specimen is noted, though the specific impact is not detailed. The prognosis is tempered by the lack of postoperative PSA or follow-up information, which is critical for assessing the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 76.69 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 46.02–107.37 months; its coverage has not been validated."
